Original commit body from cdcafb20403ff9f5f3fc8fa33fc3efef23004a36:

The tool's own warning says "Disable per run: --backend tree-sitter", in the
same message that discloses that indexing EXECUTES the analysed repository's
build.rs and proc macros as the invoking user. That flag did not disable
anything.

cli.py's argv preprocessing translated --backend into the
HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER env var but implemented only the POSITIVE arm; the
tree-sitter arm stripped the flag and set nothing. And because the flag was
stripped from argv, should_use_rust_analyzer_backend never received
backend_flag in production at all — it read only the env var, which the
opt-out never cleared. The gate was not at fault: it takes flag and environ as
separate injectable parameters and would have honoured a flag that reached it.

The failure direction is arbitrary code execution, and it bit exactly the
users who followed the only durable opt-in hypergumbo offers: export
HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1 (WI-sobig), then deliberately opt out for one repo
you do not trust, and run its build scripts anyway.

VERIFIED END-TO-END on an authored fixture (no build.rs, no deps, so arm B
never executes untrusted code):
  pre-fix   env=1 + --backend tree-sitter -> "backend ACTIVE", 10 scip nodes/26
  post-fix  env=1 + --backend tree-sitter -> "NOT enabled",     0 scip nodes/16
  control   --backend rust-analyzer       -> "backend ACTIVE", 10 scip nodes/26
Arm D's 16 nodes match the pure tree-sitter baseline exactly.

Fixed as a precedence rule with ONE owner. hypergumbo_core.backend_selection
.resolve_optin holds the ADR-0045 order (flag > env > project config > user
config > default) and is consulted by both the CLI and the gate, so the two
cannot come to disagree about what --backend tree-sitter means. Its return is
THREE-VALUED — None (no tier has an opinion) vs False (a tier said no). Both
behave identically today, so a two-valued version would pass the same tests;
it would also be a latent bug, because once a config tier lands an explicit
HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=0 collapsed into "no opinion" starts LOSING to a
config file that says on, silently re-enabling execution the user turned off.
Unobservable through any current caller, hence pinned by tests here.

Two homes closed rather than one added: the rust-analyzer selection
vocabulary moved to core (the CLI cannot import the optional lang package),
gate.py imports it instead of redeclaring it, gate._is_env_enabled /
_is_flag_enabled now DELEGATE so truthiness has one definition, and the two
duplicated --backend arms merged into one helper. The env var survives as the
TRANSPORT to a gate deep in the analyzer registry, under a documented rule:
only ever write it with a decision from a tier that OUTRANKS it, never from a
config tier it is supposed to lose to.

Two tests that pinned the old behaviour were updated, not deleted —
premise kept (flag consumed, rc == 0), conclusion inverted. Separately
recorded on INV-funam and NOT fixed here: preprocessing strips
--backend <anything> before argparse runs, so choices= never sees the global
flag and an unrecognised value is silently swallowed. One of my own new tests
asserted argparse would reject it; the test was wrong, not the code.

